    "body": "## Problem\n\n`clear` (ED 2) rewrites the visible rows in place and the extmarks on them stay where they were. The `nvim.terminal.prompt` marks that `[[`/`]]` use then point into unrelated output: after `seq 1 15`, `clear`, `seq 1 20`, `[[` jumps into the middle of the output instead of the prompt.\n\nReadline's `<C-l>` sends the same `\\e[H\\e[2J` without re-emitting OSC 133, so this cannot be handled in the `133;A` handler; only the erase itself marks the clear.\n\n## Solution\n\nExpose the erase to nvim as a vterm screen callback (next to `sb_clear`) and drop the extmarks on the erased rows when the erase covers the whole screen, outside the alternate screen. The removal is queued on the event loop in order with TermRequest events and deferred while scrollback is pending, like `emit_termrequest()`, so a prompt emitted before the erase loses its mark and one emitted after keeps it. Partial erases (`\\e[J`, `\\e[K`) are left alone.\n\nChecked with bash on a real pty (`PROMPT_COMMAND` from `:h terminal-osc133`): `clear`, readline `<C-l>` (with OSC 133 in `PS1`, readline reprints it after the erase), and output scrolled past the screen before `clear`. Tests cover the erased rows, scrollback lines, partial erases, the alternate screen, the ordering with TermRequest (same chunk and deferred), pending scrollback deletion, and `[[` after `clear`.\n\nNot in this PR: marks on scrollback lines removed by trimming or ED 3 collapse to line 1 rather than being removed (`extmark_adjust()` with `MAXLNUM`). Happy to follow up if wanted.\n\nFixes #41961\n",
    "closed_at": "2026-09-22T10:31:00Z",
